# Copy to .env and fill in your values:
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Two independent credentials, for two arms of the experiment. You only need the one
# for the arm you are running; re-scoring already-collected runs needs neither.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The comparator arm (gpt-4.1 asked to verbalize a probability vector).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint: OpenAI directly, Azure OpenAI, LiteLLM, a Bedrock
# proxy. Read by src/utils/llm_factory.py. The survey YAML (model, temperature,
# max_concurrency) overrides MODEL_NAME/TEMPERATURE at runtime.
#
# The configs ship a plain `gpt-4.1`, so these two lines as written run against stock
# OpenAI. The shipped results were collected as `azure/gpt-4.1` through a provider; point
# API_BASE_URL back at one and restore that model id to reproduce them exactly.
API_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
API_KEY=your_api_key_here

# A personal OpenAI account has far lower rate limits than a provider. The configs set
# `max_concurrency: 50`, which holds 50 whole persona-walks open at once -- lower it to
# 8-16 in the YAML and raise LLM_MAX_RETRIES below if you see 429s.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Jev arm (scripts/twin2k/probe_jev.py) — TypeSafe, NOT the endpoint above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reached directly rather than through any provider, which is why the probe is
# PUBLIC-DATA-ONLY: Twin-2K-500 is CC BY 4.0 and is the only data cleared to be sent
# there. The probe refuses any config outside configs/twin2k/, and any endpoint that is
# not TypeSafe, before a single respondent is loaded. Both refusals are tested.
#
# Either name works; JEV_KEY is an accepted alias.
# TYPESAFE_API_KEY=your_typesafe_key_here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — defaults when the survey config YAML does not set them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note the structured-output consequence before changing the model: bedrock/* routes to
# `function_calling`, because Bedrock's validator rejects array `maxItems`, while
# everything else uses the hard-enforced `json_schema`. The probability arm asks for its
# vector through the schema ALONE, so it depends on that enforcement — see
# docs/survey/04-elicitation-effects.md.
# MODEL_NAME=gpt-4.1
# TEMPERATURE=0.7
# LLM_MAX_RETRIES=2
